-# Verification report: walmart-azure-headless 1.6.4
+# Verification report: walmart-azure-headless 1.6.5
+
+## Release 1.6.5
+
+Release 1.6.5 corrects a defect of the independent source check, found when the export of this delivery's live
+census L10 (release 1.6.4) was verified. What a run collects and exports does not change: the page reader, the
+records, the gates and the exports are those of 1.6.4. An offline test on two pages that census stored shows the
+defect and its correction; each new test of the change fails on 1.6.4 and passes on 1.6.5, and the tests that show
+what did not change pass on both. Live results are not recorded here: the delivery's review documents give each
+live stage, the frozen build it ran on and its result.
+
+| Change | Until 1.6.4 | Where |
+|---|---|---|
+| The source check (`checks/source_check.py`: `validate-export --run`, and the automated canary on a run's first recorded page) reads the three price layouts that the page reader reads: price lines, nested price objects (`currentPrice`, `wasPrice`, `unitPrice` › `priceString`), and flat display strings (`priceInfo.linePriceDisplay`, `linePrice` or `itemPrice`; `priceInfo.wasPrice` and `priceInfo.unitPrice` as strings). A was-price above the current price is a sale in each layout. A record whose price or unit price differs from its page's, in any layout, is still a mismatch | The check read price lines and nested objects only. A page in the flat layout showed it no price, so a valid record read from it was a mismatch (its regular price, and its unit price when it has one), or was judged on another page that shows the item differently (its URL or image). Live L10 of 2026-11-04 (run `01M6KA3CKYJSD6WDY4TDAAV73Y`): one page, Beauty's "New Arrivals" (`/browse/beauty/new-arrivals/1085666_4427759`), gave the prices of its 50 placements as flat strings; the check reported 21 of the run's 10,301 records as mismatches (19 regular prices, 12 unit prices, 1 URL, 1 image), each equal to that page's own data, and `validate-export --run` exited 6. The canary compares a run's first recorded page in the same way, so a first page in this layout would have stopped a run (exit 6, store held) although its records were right. Of the 1,526 pages stored by the collector's 21 live runs (releases 1.5.0 to 1.6.4), it is the only page with flat price strings | `checks/source_check.py` (`expected_prices`, `flat_text`); `tests/unit/test_flat_price_layout.py` on the authentic pages `tests/captures/listing_pages_20261104_flat/` (the collector's stored copies, with their manifest) and the run's 26 exported records read from the flat page: the prices read, the 26 records verified, the record seen on both pages judged on its own, the canary's comparison, a wrong price or unit price still a mismatch, a flat was-price as a sale (6 tests: fail on 1.6.4); the manifest and the two pages' layouts (pass on both) |
 
 ## Release 1.6.4
 
